1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:15,410
Alphabet priced its first Hong Kong dollar bonds on 12 November 2026. Sixteen billion Hong Kong dollars, plus a Singapore dollar leg, about 3.8 billion US dollars together.

2
00:00:15,410 --> 00:00:36,890
MTR, the Airport Authority and HK Electric were indicated around four to four and a quarter percent. Alphabet's two-year came at 4.35, the five-year at 4.70, and the ten-year at 5.20.

3
00:00:36,890 --> 00:00:50,380
Initial thoughts were 4.60, 5.00 and 5.50. Final Hong Kong dollar yields were 25 to 30 basis points tighter on every tranche.

4
00:00:50,380 --> 00:01:06,750
Size leaned to the short end. Seven and a half billion in the two-year, seven billion in the five-year, and only one and a half billion in the ten-year.

5
00:01:06,750 --> 00:01:20,240
Hong Kong dollar orders topped 32.5 billion against a 16 billion print. Singapore dollar orders topped 4 billion against 2.28 billion.

6
00:01:20,240 --> 00:01:35,000
One-month HIBOR is about 2.68 percent. The gap versus the coupons is not a locked spread. HIBOR has spiked above 5 percent before.

7
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:54,900
Long-term debt rose from about 46.5 billion dollars to about 125 billion. Third-quarter capex of 51.4 billion ran ahead of operating cash flow.

8
00:01:54,900 --> 00:02:13,190
A one percent rise in yields takes roughly seven and a half percent off the ten-year. The yield pickup is real. It is not a free carry trade.
